  "text": "If a single writer were asked to choose a representative for a world‑poetry collection, the choice would likely be almost unanimous across the region, a rarity in that part of the world. The selected poet, whose fame was immense while alive and has not waned since his death, serves as a benchmark for judging the work of his contemporaries. To claim that another poet matches him in certain respects is to bestow high praise. Yet such comparisons often carry a note of irritation, implying that the celebrated poet’s dominance may partly stem from lucky circumstances. This sentiment is voiced most frequently by writers from a neighboring country, who hold their own late‑blooming modernist poet—who ended his career prematurely by suicide—in the highest regard."
